  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Active Scanning — TCP Port Sweep", "technique": "T1595.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-08-15 09:12:04 CEST, Suricata logged a burst of 'ET SCAN Sequential TCP Scan' alerts (signature_id 2010937) with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 traversing ports 22, 80, 443, 8080, 8443 against data.dstip=10.108.57.120 (WEBAPP-PROD-01). The scan rate (~30 pps, -T2 --max-rate 30 equivalent) is under aggressive-IPS thresholds (typically 100+ pps) but trips Suricata sequential-port signatures because the 5 ports were walked in numerical order. Duration: ~1h30m, ending around 10:42 CEST. This is the classic Phase-1 port-sweep step preceding service-version probing or direct exploitation.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET SCAN Sequential TCP Scan' (sig_id 2010937) triggered with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 and data.dstport iterating across 22, 80, 443, 8080, 8443 against data.dstip=10.108.57.120. The univariate signature: single external src + sequential walk of 5+ admin/web ports against a single internal target within a 2h window.", "first_seen": "2027-08-15T09:12:04+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Active Scanning — Vulnerability Scanner Probing", "technique": "T1595.002", "what_happened": "At 11:43:18 CEST (~1h after the TCP-scan phase ended at ~10:42, with a deliberate operator-pause for triage), the same data.srcip=203.0.113.66 initiated a web-path enumeration scan against WEBAPP-PROD-01 over HTTP (port 80). The HTTP User-Agent string was 'Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; Nessus)' — a well-known vulnerability-scanner UA. Suricata fired 'ET WEB_SERVER Possible Nessus User-Agent Detected' (signature_id 2024787) on multiple HTTP requests, with target paths spanning /admin, /api, /api/v1, /api/v1/products, /actuator/env, /server-status, /.git/config, /.env, /backup.zip, /phpinfo.php, /wp-admin, /phpmyadmin (20 distinct path probes). The probe pace was ~1 request every 4 minutes (1h20m total duration), consistent with a competent operator-paced vuln scan to avoid noisy-bot rate detection. Duration: ~1h30m, ending around 13:13 CEST.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET WEB_SERVER Possible Nessus User-Agent Detected' (sig_id 2024787) with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 → data.dstip=10.108.57.120 + data.dstport=80 on multiple HTTP URI paths. The 1-hour pause between Phase 1 (TCP port sweep) and Phase 2 (vuln-scanner enumeration) is the operator-review signature consistent with a human-driven red-team workflow, not an automated single-shot scanner.", "first_seen": "2027-08-15T11:43:18+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Exploit Public-Facing Application — SQL Injection", "technique": "T1190", "what_happened": "At 14:18:42 CEST (~1h after the vuln-scan phase ended at ~13:13, with a deliberate operator-pause to triage the discovered paths and SELECT /api/v1/products as the exploit target), the same data.srcip=203.0.113.66 launched a sequence of SQL injection payloads against http://10.108.57.120/api/v1/products?id=<payload>. Suricata fired 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION SELECT FROM' (signature_id 2006408), 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION UNION SELECT' (signature_id 2006440), and 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION Generic SQL Injection Attempt' (signature_id 2017045) on the burst of payloads. The 10 distinct payloads include classic-OR-bypass (1' OR '1'='1), UNION-based extraction (1 UNION SELECT username,password FROM users--), destructive (1; DROP TABLE users--), time-based blind (1' AND SLEEP(5)--, 1' AND BENCHMARK(1000000,SHA1(0x41))--), error-based (1' OR 1=CONVERT(int,(SELECT @@version))--), and information_schema extraction (1' UNION SELECT NULL,table_name FROM information_schema.tables--). Pace: ~1 payload every 3 minutes (~30 min total). The diversity of payload families demonstrates an operator hand-iterating variants — NOT a single sqlmap one-shot. Duration: ~30 min, ending ~14:48 CEST.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signatures 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION SELECT FROM' (sig_id 2006408), 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION UNION SELECT' (sig_id 2006440), 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION Generic' (sig_id 2017045) with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 → data.dstip=10.108.57.120 + data.dstport=80 + HTTP URI matching /api/v1/products?id=*. The narrow target path /api/v1/products (vs Phase 2's 20 candidate paths) demonstrates Phase 3 exploit selection from Phase 2 triage = methodical kill-chain progression.", "first_seen": "2027-08-15T14:18:42+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Coordinated Multi-Phase Campaign / Cumulative Volume", "technique": "T1595.001 + T1595.002 + T1190", "what_happened": "Across the 09:12-14:48 window (~5h36m), Wazuh aggregated ~138 distinct Suricata alerts with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 against data.dstip=10.108.57.120 — combining sequential-TCP-scan alerts (Phase 1, ~45 alerts), Nessus UA web-enumeration alerts (Phase 2, ~63 alerts), and SQL injection signature alerts (Phase 3, ~30 alerts). The cumulative volume + single-external-src + single-target + polite-timing-with-multiple-1h-pauses + clean-kill-chain-progression (recon → vuln-discovery → exploit) is the canonical coordinated red-team / APT-grade attack fingerprint. MITRE mapping attached: T1595.001 (Active Scanning: IP Block Scan) + T1595.002 (Vulnerability Scanning) + T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'Multi-phase reconnaissance + exploit chain from single external source' (rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'ids' + 'attack_chain') fired on aggregate detection of (a) ET SCAN signatures, (b) ET WEB_SERVER vuln-scanner signatures, AND (c) ET WEB_SPECIFIC SQL_INJECTION signatures — all from the SAME data.srcip against the SAME data.dstip within a 6-hour rolling window. The MITRE mapping T1595.001 + T1595.002 + T1190 is attached automatically by the rule.", "first_seen": "2027-08-15T14:48:00+0200"}
